{"title":"Unit vector","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><p>Unit vectors are vectors whose magnitude is exactly 1 unit. -taken from khan academy\n</p><p>In mathematics, a unit vector in a normed vector space is a vector of length 1. A unit vector is often denoted by a lowercase letter with a circumflex, or \"hat\"(<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/6/6/566aee6b0ff5e819e1ed5ac85effe461ffbc0d1f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\hat{i}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"8\" height=\"19\" />). The term normalized vector is sometimes used as a synonym for unit vector. - taken from wikipedia\n</p><p><i>This article is a stub.  Help us out by <span class=\"plainlinks\"><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=Unit_vector&amp;action=edit\">expanding it</a></span>.</i>\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018112957\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.006 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.010 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 10/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 274/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 4/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 176/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    5.630      1 Template:Stub\n100.00%    5.630      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:36697:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261018112957 and revision id 283147. Rendering was triggered because: api-parse\n -->\n</div>"}